  "system_note": "MR_26 (admit 2026-10-08 → discharge/transfer 2027-02-14). Review of the medical record against the governing DRG policies shows multiple coding/POA discrepancies requiring correction.\n\nSummary of what was billed and what the record shows (key citations):\n- Principal diagnosis billed: I21.09 (STEMI). Supported: History & Physical (Admitting), p.142 documents an acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction with cardiogenic shock on presentation (ev1: NOTE-Medical-Records-16-30-MR_26.pdf-n9, History & Physical (Admitting), p.142). Interventional Cardiology documents primary PCI to proximal LAD and residual multivessel disease (ev2: NOTE-Medical-Records-16-30-MR_26.pdf-n12, Interventional Cardiology Consultation, p.34). Discharge/Transfer Summary lists reason for admission as acute anterior STEMI with cardiogenic shock (ev3: NOTE-Medical-Records-16-30-MR_26.pdf-n1108, Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037). Principal sequencing to an acute anterior STEMI is supported by documentation (principal stays I21.x).\n\n- Cardiogenic shock (R57.0, billed POA=Y, MCC): supported. Interventional note and H&P document cardiogenic shock with placement of mechanical circulatory support (Impella CP and IABP) (ev2: Interventional Cardiology Consultation, p.34). Policy requires provider documentation and either hemodynamic instability evidence or MCS placement to validate R57.0 (ev13: RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Cardiogenic-Shock.docx-b27). The record meets those criteria (ev1, ev2).\n\n- Cardiac arrest (I46.9, billed POA=Y, MCC): not present on admission per the timeline. Discharge summary documents a pulseless ventricular tachycardia arrest on ICU day 4 with ROSC after 8 minutes and TTM (ev3: Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037). Policy for I46.9 requires documentation of a pulseless arrest event and ROSC; sequencing depends on whether arrest was present on arrival or occurred during the encounter (ev15: RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Cardiac-Arrest-with-Return-of-Spontaneous-Circulation.docx-b27). Because the arrest occurred on ICU day 4, the POA flag should be N (not present on admission), not Y as billed.\n\n- Acute kidney injury (N17.9, billed POA=N, CC): mixed timeline in the chart but provider documentation on the admitting note and nephrology consult confirm AKI related to contrast/shock. The H&P lists AKI and shows creatinine elevated on presentation (creatinine 1.4, baseline 0.9) and includes AKI on the active problem list (ev1: History & Physical (Admitting), p.142). Nephrology documents KDIGO stage 2 contrast-induced AKI with creatinine peak 2.8 and oliguria (ev4: Nephrology Consultation, p.116). Policy requires provider documentation of AKI and KDIGO objective evidence to validate N17.9 (ev16: RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Acute-Kidney-Injury.docx-b29). Because the admitting documentation already includes AKI and objective abnormal creatinine, the record supports AKI being present on admission (POA=Y) or at minimum documents it contemporaneously with initial presentation; the billed POA=N is not consistent with the admitting H&P evidence. (Change POA to Y is supported by ev1 and ev4.)\n\n- Acute respiratory failure with hypoxia (J96.01, billed POA=Y, MCC): supported. H&P and interventional progress notes document severe hypoxemia (ABG PaO2 62 on NRB; SpO2 88%) and need for invasive airway/mechanical ventilation; discharge and progress notes document intubation for pulmonary edema and subsequent trach-collar tolerance (ev9: History & Physical (Admitting), p.142; ev7 & ev8: Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037). Policy for J96.01 allows presumption of objective hypoxemia when invasive mechanical ventilation is initiated and attributed to respiratory failure (ev14: RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Acute-Respiratory-Failure-with-Hypoxia.docx-b27 and RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Acute-Respiratory-Failure-with-Hypoxia.docx-b37). J96.01 as billed appears supported and POA=Y is reasonable.\n\n- New-onset post-operative atrial fibrillation (I48.91, billed POA=N, CC): supported as hospital-acquired. Discharge summary documents 'new-onset atrial fibrillation post-cardiotomy controlled with amiodarone' (ev10: Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037). Policy requires provider documentation of new-onset post-op AF and documentation of management (ev15: RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Atrial-Fibrillation.docx-b27). POA=N is appropriate.\n\n- Procedure-related infection and MSSA (T81.4XXA, T81.89XA, B95.61 billed POA=N): supported. Discharge diagnoses and hospital course identify a superficial sternal wound infection with MSSA treated with cefazolin and wound VAC (ev5: Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037). These are hospital-acquired (POA=N) and supported by documentation.\n\n- Deep venous thrombosis (I82.402 billed = left lower extremity): discharge and transfer notes specify a right popliteal DVT on apixaban (ev5: Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037). The billed left-sided DVT (I82.402) is not supported; the correct reported location is right popliteal DVT. The billed code should be revised to the right-sided DVT code (recode I82.402 → I82.401, see code_decisions). Policy-level side/late specification issues aside, the documentation clearly identifies the DVT on the right, not left.\n\n- Do-Not-Resuscitate (Z66, billed POA=Y): unsupported. The Patient Facesheet on admission documents 'Code Status Full Code' (ev6: Patient Facesheet, p.4). No contemporaneous documentation supports a DNR in the chart; palliative consulted but family elected to proceed with aggressive interventions (ev11: Palliative Care Consultation, p.334). Remove Z66 from the coded list.\n\nProcedures (summary):\n- PCI to proximal LAD, Impella CP placement, and IABP placement are documented (ev2: Interventional Cardiology Consultation, p.34; ev7: Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037). CABG 3-vessel with LIMA to LAD and SVG to OM1 and RCA is documented (ev3: Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037). Intubation and prolonged ventilation including trach collar tolerance are documented (ev7, ev8), which supports mechanical ventilation >96 hours (5A1D90Z). Continuous cardiac output monitoring / mixed venous monitoring documented on admission orders supports 5A1221Z (ev11: History & Physical (Admitting), p.142).\n\nUnresolved conflict requiring human review:\n- Operative/procedure-side discrepancy for saphenous vein harvest (billing lists excision of right saphenous vein, 06BQ0ZZ, while another operative line in the chart indicates left saphenous harvest). The record contains conflicting lines flagged as CONFLICT-00194; the dataset does not include a resolved operative note we can rely on. I recommend human verification of the operative record to confirm the harvest side and correct ICD-10-PCS laterality (see hitl_question: CONFLICT-00194). Because the operative laterality cannot be resolved from the retrieved rows, this single-item ambiguity is escalated to HITL despite the overall DRG-level finding.\n\nNet action taken in this audit (claim-level):\n- Finding: Billing requires correction for POA flags (I46.9 should be POA=N; N17.9 POA should be Y per admitting H&P and nephrology documentation), removal of unsupported diagnosis Z66 (Do not resuscitate), and recoding of left lower-extremity DVT (I82.402) to right-sided DVT (I82.401) per discharge documentation. All other billed diagnoses and procedures are supported by the record and applicable DRG policy criteria (citations above).\n\nEvidence relied upon (evidence IDs shown inline above): ev1 (History & Physical (Admitting), p.142), ev2 (Interventional Cardiology Consultation, p.34), ev3 (Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037), ev4 (Nephrology Consultation, p.116), ev5 (Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037), ev6 (Patient Facesheet, p.4), ev7 (Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037), ev8 (Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037), ev9 (History & Physical (Admitting), p.142), ev10 (Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037), ev11 (Palliative Care Consultation, p.334), ev12 (History & Physical (Admitting), p.434), ev13 (RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Cardiogenic-Shock.docx-b27), ev14 (RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Acute-Respiratory-Failure-with-Hypoxia.docx-b27), ev15 (RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Cardiac-Arrest-with-Return-of-Spontaneous-Circulation.docx-b27), ev16 (RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Acute-Kidney-Injury.docx-b29).",
  "rationale_letter": "Re: DRG audit of inpatient claim MR_26 (admit 10/08/2026 – transfer 02/14/2027)\n\nDear Billing/Medical Records Team,\n\nWe completed a clinical review of the medical record and coding for the above inpatient stay. The chart documents an acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction with cardiogenic shock requiring primary PCI, mechanical circulatory support (Impella CP and IABP), emergency 3-vessel CABG, prolonged mechanical ventilation, and a complicated postoperative course including targeted temperature management after a pulseless ventricular tachycardia arrest, contrast-induced acute kidney injury, a superficial sternal wound infection due to methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus, and a catheter-associated right popliteal deep venous thrombosis (transfer/ discharge summary, Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037; ev3, ev5).\n\nFinding: Based on chart review and applicable DRG policies, the claim contains coding errors that require correction.\n\nBasis for Finding (specific changes required):\n1) I46.9 (Cardiac arrest) — POA indicator incorrect. The cardiac arrest is documented as occurring on ICU day 4 (p.1037) after admission (ev3). Per the Cardiac Arrest policy, I46.x is sequenced/POA per the documented timing; because arrest occurred during the hospitalization, POA should be N, not Y (RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Cardiac-Arrest-with-Return-of-Spontaneous-Circulation.docx-b27; ev15). We will change POA to N.\n\n2) N17.9 (Acute kidney failure) — POA indicator should be reviewed/changed to Y. The admitting History & Physical documents acute kidney injury with an elevated creatinine on presentation and includes AKI on the active problems (History & Physical (Admitting), p.142; ev1). Nephrology documents KDIGO stage 2 contrast-induced AKI with creatinine peak 2.8 (Nephrology Consultation, p.116; ev4). Policy requires provider documentation plus KDIGO objective criteria to validate N17.9 (RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Acute-Kidney-Injury.docx-b29; ev16). Given the admitting documentation and objective labs, N17.9 should be POA=Y (present on admission) rather than POA=N.\n\n3) I82.402 (Acute embolism and thrombosis of unspecified deep veins of left lower extremity) — side/location mismatch. The Discharge / Transfer Summary documents a right popliteal DVT on apixaban (ev5). The billed left-sided DVT is not supported; the code should be changed to the appropriate right-sided DVT code (recode to I82.401 per the chart) to reflect the documented right popliteal DVT.\n\n4) Z66 (Do not resuscitate) — unsupported by the contemporaneous record. The Patient Facesheet (admit) documents Code Status: Full Code (Patient Facesheet, p.4; ev6) and the palliative consult documents goals-of-care discussion with family electing to proceed (Palliative Care Consultation, p.334; ev11). There is no contemporaneous DNR order or documentation authorizing Z66; remove Z66 from the claim.\n\n5) Operative laterality verification (HITL): the operative record contains conflicting lines regarding the side of saphenous-vein harvest vs. the billed PCS (06BQ0ZZ = excision of right saphenous vein). This conflict was flagged in the chart (CONFLICT-00194). Please review and confirm the operative note/OR record to determine the correct laterality and, if needed, the correct ICD-10-PCS code for excision/harvest of the saphenous vein.\n\nCorrection to the claim: the auditing team will (a) change POA for I46.9 to N, (b) change POA for N17.9 to Y, (c) recode I82.402 to the right-sided DVT code (I82.401) per the discharge summary, and (d) remove Z66. All other billed diagnoses and procedures were supported by provider documentation and applicable DRG policy criteria (e.g., cardiogenic shock, acute respiratory failure with hypoxia, mechanical ventilation >96 hours, Impella/IABP placement, CABG). Please provide the operative record/OR nursing note for verification of saphenous-vein harvest laterality so we can finalize the PCS procedure code for vein excision if a laterality-specific PCS applies.\n\nSincerely,\nClinical Audit Team\n(Documentation and policy citations: History & Physical (Admitting), p.142; Interventional Cardiology Consultation, p.34; Nephrology Consultation, p.116; Discharge / Transfer Summary, p.1037; Patient Facesheet, p.4; RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Cardiogenic-Shock.docx-b27; RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Acute-Respiratory-Failure-with-Hypoxia.docx-b27; RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Cardiac-Arrest-with-Return-of-Spontaneous-Circulation.docx-b27; RULE-Policy-Documents-DRG-Acute-Kidney-Injury.docx-b29.)",
